<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Robots often rely on RGB images for tasks like manipulation and navigation. However, reliable interaction typically requires a 3D scene representation that is metric-scaled and aligned with the robot reference frame. This depends on accurate camera-to-robot calibration and dense 3D reconstruction, tasks usually treated separately, despite both relying on geometric correspondences from RGB data. Traditional calibration needs patterns, while RGB-based reconstruction yields geometry with an unknown scale in an arbitrary frame. Multi-camera setups add further complexity, as data must be expressed in a shared reference frame.
We present Calib3R, a patternless method that jointly performs camera-to-robot calibration and metric-scaled 3D reconstruction via unified optimization. Calib3R handles single- and multi-camera setups on robot arms or mobile robots. It builds on the 3D foundation model MASt3R to extract pointmaps from RGB images, which are combined with robot poses to reconstruct a scaled 3D scene aligned with the robot. Experiments on diverse datasets show that Calib3R achieves accurate calibration with less than 10 images, outperforming target-less and marker-based methods.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>Calibration and Identification; Sensor Fusion; 3D Reconstruction.

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">INTRODUCTION</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Robots perceive and understand their surroundings using onboard sensors, most commonly RGB or RGB-D cameras, which capture both visual and geometric information from the environment for tasks such as manipulation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> and autonomous navigation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.
Although RGB-D cameras and other 3D sensors provide depth information, they often suffer from noise and incomplete measurements in real-world conditions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.
RGB cameras, on the other hand, are generally more stable, less affected by noise and capable of capturing high-resolution images rich in color and texture. For this reason, many robotics applications prefer to rely on RGB data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.
However, despite its effectiveness, 2D visual data alone is insufficient for safe and reliable interaction with the environment. Robots require a 3D representation of their surroundings that is metric-scaled and aligned with their own reference frame <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.08813v1/Calib3R_new.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/511;" width="476" height="511" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 1: </span>Overview of the Calib3R method. A set of RGB images and their corresponding robot poses are used as input. The images are processed by the 3D foundation model MASt3R to generate dense local pointmaps. These pointmaps, together with the robot poses, are then used in a joint optimization that estimates the global scene geometry, performs camera-to-robot calibration, and refines cross-camera alignment. The result is a metric-scaled 3D reconstruction aligned with the robot reference frame.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This challenge becomes even more complex when multiple RGB cameras are used simultaneously to cover a wider field of view and capture richer visual information, as all camera data must be expressed with respect to the robot reference frame <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.
Addressing this issue involves two fundamental tasks: (i) extrinsic calibration of the onboard cameras with respect to the robot’s reference frame, and
(ii) 3D scene reconstruction, to estimate the environment’s geometry from visual inputs.
Although both processes rely on geometric correspondences extracted from a set of RGB images, they are traditionally addressed as separate tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the context of extrinsic calibration, the process is commonly referred to as hand-eye calibration for robotic arms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, and as motion-based calibration for mobile robots <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. Despite the differences between these two approaches, they share a common goal: estimating the pose of each camera with respect to a robot reference frame
such as the end-effector frame for manipulators or the base frame for mobile robots.
To encompass these configurations under a unified term, we refer to the general case as <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">camera-to-robot calibration</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Traditional camera-to-robot calibration methods typically rely on capturing multiple images of fiducial markers or calibration patterns <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, require dedicated infrastructure such as external motion capture system <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, additional sensors such as LiDAR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, and often involve manual data collection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. These requirements make them too slow and cumbersome for dynamic environments where fast and frequent recalibration is often required. Moreover, calibration techniques for multi-camera setups typically focus on relative extrinsics between cameras, while neglecting the estimation of the transformation between each camera and the robot reference frame <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>.
Additionally, most existing approaches are tailored to either robotic arms or mobile platforms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, neglecting the shared challenges between these settings.
Such limitations highlight the growing need for calibration methods that are fast, robust, and easy to deploy, and that can seamlessly generalize across different robotic configurations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Once the camera-to-robot calibration is available, the second step—reconstructing the 3D scene—typically relies on multi-view geometry.
Approaches like DUSt3R <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> and its successor MASt3R <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> propose a novel two-view reconstruction paradigm that estimates local dense 3D geometry directly from two RGB images by extracting visual correspondences and relative camera poses.
MASt3R-SfM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> further extends this approach by introducing a global alignment strategy that optimizes multiple pairwise poses to place all views into a shared coordinate system, even though only up to an unknown scale factor.
While the resulting reconstructions lack the metric scale, their geometric accuracy and consistent alignment offer a valuable opportunity: by adding the information provided by the robot’s known poses (e.g., from odometry or forward kinematics), it is possible to simultaneously estimate the camera-to-robot transformation and the scale factor required to align the reconstruction to the robot reference frame, thus obtaining a metric 3D representation of the environment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To exploit this opportunity, we propose Calib3R, the first method to jointly perform camera-to-robot calibration and dense 3D reconstruction using only RGB images and corresponding robot poses, all within a single comprehensive optimization process.
Images captured by a single- or multi-camera system mounted on the robot are first processed by the 3D foundation model MASt3R, which produces local pointmaps. These pointmaps, combined with the robot’s poses, are used in a unified optimization that reconstructs the global scene geometry, performs camera-to-robot calibration, and, in the multi-camera case, refines the relative alignment between cameras. The result is a metric-scaled 3D reconstruction aligned with the robot’s reference frame, as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I INTRODUCTION ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Calib3R demonstrated to be robust and accurate over a wide range of synthetic and real-world scenarios, including both robotic manipulators and mobile robots, eliminating the need for depth sensors, fiducial markers, or multi-stage calibration procedures and achieving high-precision calibration with less than 10 images. Notably, Calib3R outperformed existing target-less methods and even several pattern-based baselines.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, this paper presents the following key contributions:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Calib3R, a unified framework for joint camera-to-robot calibration and metric-scaled 3D scene reconstruction in robotic systems with multiple cameras, including both mobile platforms and manipulators;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">a joint loss function that integrates relative local reconstructions from a 3D foundation model with robot motion constraints, enabling the estimation of all main camera parameters in a single optimization step without requiring any calibration pattern;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">an extensive performance evaluation on diverse datasets, demonstrating that Calib3R achieves a precision level comparable to or better than pattern-based methods, while being more general;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">a systematic study on data efficiency, showing that Calib3R remains robust and outperforms both targetless and pattern-based baselines even in low-data scenarios.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">RELATED WORKS</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hand-Eye Calibration:</span>
Hand-eye calibration is a well-established problem in robotics that aims to estimate the relative pose between a camera and a robot’s end-effector. It is typically addressed by capturing a series of images of a known calibration pattern (e.g., a checkerboard) using a camera rigidly mounted on the robot hand, and using both the images and the corresponding robot poses to compute the camera’s extrinsic parameters. Different mathematical formulations exist for solving hand-eye calibration; a widely adopted approach involves solving the equation <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AX=XB" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AX=XB</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is the unknown rigid transformation describing the pose of the camera with respect to the robot, while <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> denote the relative motions of the end-effector (from robot kinematics) and the camera (from pattern observations), respectively <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>.
Several other approaches were proposed: Shah <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> formulated a closed-form solution for the hand-eye problem by using an algorithm based on Singular Value Decomposition (SVD) and the Kronecker product to solve for rotation and translation separately, while Li et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> used dual quaternions to solve them simultaneously overcoming the limitations of the Kronecker product. Wang et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> extended hand-eye calibration to multi-camera setups by incorporating a common reference frame but required an external motion capture system, limiting its applicability to small setups.
Andreff and Heller <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> proposed two similar hand-eye calibration methods that leverage the Structure-from-Motion (SfM) paradigm to estimate camera motion and introduce a formulation for hand-eye calibration that includes a factor to metrically scale camera poses. However, their methods treat motion estimation and calibration as two separate steps. This decoupling, especially when relying on hand-crafted features such as SIFT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> for point correspondences, can lead to suboptimal calibration accuracy and reduced robustness.
Furthermore, although their approaches achieve metric-scaled camera poses, they do not produce a globally consistent 3D reconstruction aligned with the robot’s reference frame in a unified process.
More recent learning-based approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> introduce deep models for hand-eye calibration but require the robot’s gripper to be visible in the camera view.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Zhi et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> were the first to leverage the 3D foundation model DUSt3R for hand-eye calibration of a single-camera system mounted on a robot arm, but their method, Joint Calibration and Representation (JCR), relies on a precomputed reconstruction that is aligned to the robot only in a subsequent post-processing step. As a consequence, reconstruction and calibration remain two separate processes, with the 3D model treated as an external input rather than being integrated into the calibration loop. This separation limits the mutual refinement between the two stages and ultimately reduces the accuracy and generality of the results. Additionally, JCR performs translation and rotation optimization independently, a decoupled strategy that reduces accuracy. Finally, the method relies on a grid search over a predefined range of scale values to determine the optimal metric factor—an approach that is computationally inefficient and not well suited for general, scalable applications.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We address these limitations by introducing a unified optimization framework that jointly performs multi-camera to robot calibration and metric-scaled 3D reconstruction at once. The method integrates the 3D foundation model directly into the calibration process, allowing both stages to benefit from mutual feedback. Crucially, it also optimizes the metric scale factor as part of the same loop, eliminating the need for exhaustive grid search.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Motion-Based Calibration:</span> Although often treated separately, motion-based calibration is fundamentally related to hand-eye calibration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>. Both share the same underlying goal: estimating the transformation <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> that best aligns the motion of the camera with that of the robot. In this context, <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> represent the relative motions of the robot base and the camera, respectively. The robot base frame is typically defined at the geometric center of the four wheels, and its motion is commonly derived from odometry or wheel encoder measurements. Censi et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> exploit this approach by combining wheel odometry with exteroceptive sensor data for multi-sensor calibration on mobile robots. Similarly, Kummerle et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite> propose an algorithm for visual odometry calibration based on motion data.
CamOdoCal by Heng et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite> calibrates multi-camera systems integrating wheel odometry with matching features between current frames and all other cameras’ past frames, leading to slow and computationally intensive calibration.
Della Corte et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> build on these efforts by jointly estimating sensor time delays and extrinsic parameters between cameras and robot. However, it is well known that motion-based methods struggle to recover the full six degrees of freedom (6DoF) in camera-to-robot transformations when the robot moves on a planar surface <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>. In such cases, the z-component of the transformation becomes unobservable due to the lack of vertical motion, a limitation known as the unobservability of the z-coordinate.
To address this, Zuniga’s RobotAutocalib <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite> rely on ground plane detection to estimate all 6DoF. However, its sequential two-step process, first solving for <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="yaw" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">yaw</annotation></semantics></math> angle, then estimating the remaining 3DoF, can introduce inconsistencies. It also requires a large number of images (often exceeding 50) to accurately reconstruct the ground plane. Similarly, MEMROC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> achieves full 6DoF estimation with fewer images but depends on a calibration pattern for extrinsic estimation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, several learning-based calibration methods have emerged. Meyer et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite> introduced a technique that uses road features (e.g., lane markings) to calibrate yaw and estimate ground orientation for pitch and roll. However, this method assumes the vehicle follows a straight path with clearly defined road markings. Yan et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite> proposed SensorX2Car, which estimates the 3DoF rotation between the sensor and the robot using vanishing points and the horizon line. This method, however, assumes the vehicle’s orientation aligns with its trajectory, making it unsuitable for side-mounted cameras <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>.
To address these limitations, our novel calibration method estimates the full 6DoF pose of single- or multi-camera systems with respect to the robot by detecting the ground plane directly from the metric-scaled 3D reconstruction which is also generated by our method. This approach does not require external markers or other scene elements such as lane markings, vanishing points, or predefined trajectories.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3D Foundation Models:</span> Traditional 3D reconstruction methods, such as Structure-from-Motion (SfM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite> and Multi-View Stereo (MVS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite>, rely heavily on visual feature matching across multiple views. While learning-based methods such as VGGSfM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite> and DF-SfM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite> have advanced the field by integrating end-to-end trainable components into the classical SfM pipeline, they still depend on handcrafted solutions for keypoint detection, camera initialization, and triangulation. As a result, they often struggle in scenarios with only a few available viewpoints or minimal camera motion.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The emergence of large-scale 3D Foundation Models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>, <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite> has marked a paradigm shift in 3D vision. Among these, DUSt3R <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> decomposes the SfM problem into two distinct stages: (1) local 3D reconstruction from image pairs through pairwise pointmaps, and (2) global alignment of these pointmaps into a unified world coordinate system. Although this formulation simplifies the overall optimization process compared to traditional pipelines, it introduces challenges in precision and scalability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">To overcome these limitations, MASt3R <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> extends DUSt3R by incorporating dense, pixel-level matching and more robust pointmap regression mechanisms, leading to more reliable local reconstructions. Building further on this foundation, MASt3R-SfM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> proposes a complete multi-view framework, processing multiple image pairs with MASt3R and subsequently aligning them to a consistent global coordinate frame through a dedicated optimization process.
More recently, VGGT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite>, a state-of-the-art 3D foundation model, introduced a feed-forward neural network to directly infer all key 3D scene attributes without requiring any explicit optimization step.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Despite these advancements, a critical limitation of current 3D foundation models is their lack of metric consistency and alignment with a robot’s reference frame. These models rely solely on independently moving cameras without explicitly including robot motion into the reconstruction pipeline. As highlighted in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, they often fail to produce physically accurate, metric-scaled reconstructions, even when trained on metrically consistent data. This scale ambiguity, combined with the absence of alignment to a robot’s reference frame, significantly hinders the applicability of these 3D foundation models in robotics, where accurate metric reconstruction is essential. Calib3R addresses these limitations by directly integrating robot poses into the optimization process, enabling both metric-scaled 3D reconstruction and accurate camera-to-robot calibration. This joint formulation ensures that the resulting 3D model is not only metrically consistent but also aligned to the robot’s reference frame.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Preliminaries</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our method takes inspiration from the recent MASt3R-SfM framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, which performs Structure-from-Motion optimization based on pointmaps output by the 3D foundation model MASt3R <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>.
In this section, these models are briefly described highlighting their main mathematical concepts and notations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MASt3R</span> is a recent 3D foundation model which computes joint local 3D reconstruction and dense pixel-wise matching between a pair of input images. Formally, given two RGB images of size <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\times H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\times H</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted as <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}^{j}\in\mathbb{R}^{W\times H\times 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}^{j}\in\mathbb{R}^{W\times H\times 3}</annotation></semantics></math>, the model predicts two corresponding pointmaps, <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}^{i,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}^{i,i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}^{i,j}\in\mathbb{R}^{W\times H\times 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}^{i,j}\in\mathbb{R}^{W\times H\times 3}</annotation></semantics></math>.
These pointmaps are dense 2D maps with the same structure as the input images, where each pixel, instead of storing an RGB color, contains its corresponding 3D coordinate expressed in the reference frame of <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each pointmap is associated with its own confidence map, <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}^{i,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐂</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}^{i,i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}^{i,j}\in\mathbb{R}^{W\times H\times 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐂</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}^{i,j}\in\mathbb{R}^{W\times H\times 1}</annotation></semantics></math> which indicate the reliability of the predicted 3D points. Additionally, the model outputs two <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional features maps <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D}^{i,i},\mathbf{D}^{i,j}\in\mathbb{R}^{W\times H\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐃</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐃</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D}^{i,i},\mathbf{D}^{i,j}\in\mathbb{R}^{W\times H\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, capturing local appearance features.
To establish reliable correspondences between the two images, the fast nearest-neighbor (NN) algorithm from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> is applied to the feature maps <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D}^{i,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐃</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D}^{i,i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D}^{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐃</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D}^{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math>. This algorithm initializes a set of points on a regular pixel grid and iteratively refines them to obtain the correspondences <math id="S3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{i,j}=\{y_{p}^{i}\leftrightarrow y_{p}^{j}\}_{p=1,\dots,|\mathcal{M}^{i,j}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">↔</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{i,j}=\{y_{p}^{i}\leftrightarrow y_{p}^{j}\}_{p=1,\dots,|\mathcal{M}^{i,j}|}</annotation></semantics></math> between <math id="S3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{p}^{i},y_{p}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{p}^{i},y_{p}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a pair of matching pixels.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MASt3R-SfM</span> builds upon the MASt3R model to develop an unconstrained SfM pipeline for multi-view reconstruction from a collection of images. It exploits MASt3R’s encoder as an image matcher to identify pairs of overlapping images, and its decoder to perform pairwise local 3D reconstruction in the form of pointmaps, along with dense pixel-wise correspondences for the selected pairs. Based on the resulting local pointmaps, it performs a two-stage global optimization: first aligning local reconstructions in 3D space via a matching loss, then refining the alignment by minimizing 2D reprojection errors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, given a collection of <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> images, MASt3R-SfM first constructs a sparse scene graph <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E})</annotation></semantics></math> where each
vertex in <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> is an image and each edge <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=(n,m)\in\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=(n,m)\in\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> is a pair of overlapping images <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>.
To select this subset of informative image pairs, it adopts a scalable pairwise image matcher which predicts co-visibility scores between image pairs to retain only those with sufficient overlap.
The matcher builds on the ASMK (Aggregated Selective Match Kernels) image retrieval method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite>, leveraging the token features produced by MASt3R’s encoder as local features, to predict an approximate co-visibility score <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> that measures the likelihood of overlapping content between two images, <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By evaluating all possible image pairs, a symmetric co-visibility score matrix <math id="S3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\in[0,1]^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\in[0,1]^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math> is constructed. MASt3R-SfM then builds a sparse, connected co-visibility graph by sampling a minimal yet connected set of image pairs from <math id="S3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, via farthest point sampling and nearest-neighbor connections.
For each image pair <math id="S3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> in the co-visibility graph, MASt3R’s decoder is used for joint local 3D reconstruction and pixel-wise matching, obtaining raw pointmaps <math id="S3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}^{i,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}^{i,i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}^{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}^{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since each pair involving a same image <math id="S3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> provides a different estimate of <math id="S3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}^{n,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}^{n,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}^{n,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐂</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}^{n,n}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,j)\in\mathcal{E}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,j)\in\mathcal{E}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{n}=\{(a,b)\in\mathcal{E}|a=n\vee b=n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{n}=\{(a,b)\in\mathcal{E}|a=n\vee b=n\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the various estimates obtained for an image <math id="S3.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are aggregated into a canonical pointmap <math id="S3.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{X}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{X}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> using a confidence-weighted average defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{X}}^{n}=\frac{\sum_{e\in\mathcal{E}^{n}}\mathbf{C}^{n,n}(e)\,\mathbf{X}^{n,n}(e)}{\sum_{e\in\mathcal{E}^{n}}\mathbf{C}^{n,n}(e)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>𝐂</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>𝐂</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{X}}^{n}=\frac{\sum_{e\in\mathcal{E}^{n}}\mathbf{C}^{n,n}(e)\,\mathbf{X}^{n,n}(e)}{\sum_{e\in\mathcal{E}^{n}}\mathbf{C}^{n,n}(e)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}^{n,n}(e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}^{n,n}(e)</annotation></semantics></math> is the pointmap <math id="S3.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}^{n,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}^{n,n}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained from the image pair <math id="S3.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=(n,j)\in\mathcal{E}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=(n,j)\in\mathcal{E}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
MASt3R-SfM aims to compute global scene reconstruction, which strongly depends on parameters such as the camera intrinsics <math id="S3.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> and the extrinsic pose of each view, denoted as <math id="S3.p4.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{T}^{\mathcal{W}}_{\mathcal{C}_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{T}^{\mathcal{W}}_{\mathcal{C}_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S3.p4.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0,\dots,N-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0,\dots,N-1</annotation></semantics></math>. To ensure that the pointmaps precisely satisfy the pinhole projection model, it introduces a <em id="S3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">constrained pointmap</em> <math id="S3.p4.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\chi}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\chi}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> defined explicitly as a function of the camera parameters:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\chi}^{n}=\pi_{n}^{-1}(\sigma_{n},K,T^{\mathcal{W}}_{\mathcal{C}_{n}},\tilde{Z}^{n})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\chi}^{n}=\pi_{n}^{-1}(\sigma_{n},K,T^{\mathcal{W}}_{\mathcal{C}_{n}},\tilde{Z}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where each 3D point <math id="S3.p4.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\chi}^{n}_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\chi}^{n}_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> at pixel <math id="S3.p4.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> in the constrained pointmap is obtained by means of the inverse reprojection function <math id="S3.p4.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{n}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{n}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> depending on 3D points from the depth map <math id="S3.p4.m30" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tilde{Z}^{n}=\tilde{\mathbf{X}}^{n}_{:,:,3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{Z}^{n}=\tilde{\mathbf{X}}^{n}_{:,:,3}</annotation></semantics></math>, the camera intrinsics <math id="S3.p4.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, the camera pose of view <math id="S3.p4.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p4.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{T}^{\mathcal{W}}_{\mathcal{C}_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{T}^{\mathcal{W}}_{\mathcal{C}_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a scale factor <math id="S3.p4.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
These parameters are treated as optimization variables for each pointmap to enforce relative consistency across views through a two-step optimization process that addresses the global alignment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">First, the optimization coarsely aligns each local pointmap <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\chi}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\chi}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> within a common world coordinate system <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math> by minimizing a 3D matching loss <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{3D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{3D}</annotation></semantics></math> via gradient descent. This loss reduces the distance between corresponding 3D points across multiple views and is defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{3D}=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}p\in\mathcal{M}^{(n,m)}\\
(n,m)\in\mathcal{E}\end{subarray}}q_{p}\,\left|\left|\mathbf{\chi}^{n}_{p}-\mathbf{\chi}^{m}_{p}\right|\right|_{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{3D}=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}p\in\mathcal{M}^{(n,m)}\\
(n,m)\in\mathcal{E}\end{subarray}}q_{p}\,\left|\left|\mathbf{\chi}^{n}_{p}-\mathbf{\chi}^{m}_{p}\right|\right|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\mathcal{M}^{n,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\mathcal{M}^{n,m}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the matching pixels in each respective image, weighted by their respective confidence <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Then, a second optimization stage refines the scene geometry by minimizing the 2D reprojection error of 3D points in all image pairs, represented by the reprojection loss <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{2D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{2D}</annotation></semantics></math> defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{2D}=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}p\in\mathcal{M}^{(n,m)}\\
(n,m)\in\mathcal{E}\end{subarray}}q_{p}\left(\left|\left|y_{p}^{n}-\pi_{n}(\mathbf{\chi}^{m}_{p})\right|\right|_{2}+\left|\left|y_{p}^{m}-\pi_{m}(\mathbf{\chi}^{n}_{p})\right|\right|_{2}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{2D}=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}p\in\mathcal{M}^{(n,m)}\\
(n,m)\in\mathcal{E}\end{subarray}}q_{p}\left(\left|\left|y_{p}^{n}-\pi_{n}(\mathbf{\chi}^{m}_{p})\right|\right|_{2}+\left|\left|y_{p}^{m}-\pi_{m}(\mathbf{\chi}^{n}_{p})\right|\right|_{2}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.2" class="ltx_p">ensuring that projected 3D points <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{n}(\mathbf{\chi}^{m}_{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{n}(\mathbf{\chi}^{m}_{p})</annotation></semantics></math> from camera <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> accurately correspond to their original 2D observations <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{p}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{p}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> on camera <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and viceversa, with each term weighted by the confidence <math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> of the pixel correspondence between <math id="S3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{p}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{p}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{p}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{p}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">However, this two-step sequential approach is prone to error accumulation,
making it less reliable than joint optimization methods. Moreover, MASt3R-SfM exhibits physical scale inconsistencies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, producing 3D representations that lack metric accuracy and are not aligned with the robot’s reference frame, posing significant challenges for deployment in real-world robotics scenarios.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Calib3R</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Calib3R jointly performs camera-to-robot calibration and 3D scene reconstruction, supporting a wide range of robotic systems equipped with RGB cameras, including both mobile platforms and manipulators,
as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I INTRODUCTION ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Calib3R follows the approach presented in MASt3R-SfM, making use of the foundation model MASt3R to compute pairwise local 3D pointmaps, which are then aligned through a global optimization process into a common reference frame.
The proposed method handles both single- and multi-camera setups mounted on a robot, and relies on a unified optimization step that incorporates robot-centric information—such as kinematics or odometry—to improve reconstruction consistency and produce metric-scaled, globally consistent 3D reconstructions directly aligned with the robot’s coordinate system.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ IV Calib3R ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, consider a mobile robot navigating an environment or a manipulator operating within its workspace, equipped with <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> RGB cameras. While moving through a sequence of <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> robot poses, the system captures images of a generic static 3D scene. Given this collection of <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\times M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\times M</annotation></semantics></math> images, the objective is to estimate the transformation <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\mathcal{R}}_{\mathcal{C}_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\mathcal{R}}_{\mathcal{C}_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> between each camera <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and a robot reference frame <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., robot end-effector or robot base) while simultaneously producing a metric 3D reconstruction of the observed scene, expressed in the initial robot reference frame (<math id="S4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}=\mathcal{R}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}=\mathcal{R}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>).
To achieve this, Calib3R first computes pairwise local 3D pointmaps as intermediate representations, exploiting the co-visibility graph strategy introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>. As described in Section <a href="#S3" title="III Preliminaries ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>, a subset of informative image pairs <math id="S4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> is selected using a pairwise image matcher based on MASt3R’s encoder, potentially including pairs from different cameras if they have overlapping content. MASt3R then processes these pairs to generate local 3D reconstructions and computes canonical pointmaps <math id="S4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{X}}^{i}_{(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{X}}^{i}_{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math> for each image <math id="S4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=0,\dots,N-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=0,\dots,N-1</annotation></semantics></math> from each camera <math id="S4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=0,\dots,M-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=0,\dots,M-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
These local reconstructions are then aligned in the robot reference frame through a single optimization process that aims to minimize a loss function involving three main contributions: (i) a scene geometry term to enforce 3D reconstruction quality, (ii) a camera-to-robot calibration term to incorporate robot motion and metric-scaled information, and (iii) a cross-camera term to model rigidity constraints between sensors when multiple cameras are available.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">scene geometry</span> term estimates the rigid transformations
<math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\mathcal{W}}_{\mathcal{C}_{j,i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\mathcal{W}}_{\mathcal{C}_{j,i}}</annotation></semantics></math> that align each camera view <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{j,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{j,i}</annotation></semantics></math> to a common reference frame <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math>, based on 3D correspondences between local pointmaps. This term is composed of the two losses introduced in Section <a href="#S3" title="III Preliminaries ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>: the 3D matching loss <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{3D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{3D}</annotation></semantics></math> defined in (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In III Preliminaries ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) to minimize distance of any pair of matching 3D point in the pointmaps, and the 2D reprojection loss <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{2D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{2D}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In III Preliminaries ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) which enforce consistency between different viewpoints.
Since Calib3R is designed for general multi-camera setups, these constraints are explicitly modeled through per-camera losses <math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{3D,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{3D,j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{2D,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{2D,j}</annotation></semantics></math> for each generic <math id="S4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> camera.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.08813v1/calib_scheme_ax_xb.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/182;" width="476" height="182" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 2: </span>Formulation of camera-to-robot calibration. Let <math id="S4.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the robot’s reference frame at time step <math id="S4.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, either the robot base for a mobile platform (left) or the end-effector for a manipulator arm (right). The robot is equipped with one or more cameras <math id="S4.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and moves from pose <math id="S4.F2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.F2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i+1</annotation></semantics></math> within the environment, capturing images of a static 3D scene during its motion. <math id="S4.F2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{j,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{j,i}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the relative motions of the robot and the <math id="S4.F2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> camera, while <math id="S4.F2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the unknown rototranslation matrix describing the pose of the camera with respect to the robot reference frame.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">camera-to-robot calibration</span> term is a loss defined according to the classic hand-eye calibration formulation:</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}\left|\left|A_{i}X-XB_{i}\right|\right|_{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}\left|\left|A_{i}X-XB_{i}\right|\right|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p5.2" class="ltx_p">As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ IV Calib3R ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are the robot and camera motions between poses <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i+1</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is the unknown rigid transformation from the camera to the robot reference frame.
In traditional hand-eye calibration, both <math id="S4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are assumed to share the same metric scale—typically ensured by deriving <math id="S4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> from observations of a calibration pattern. In contrast, Calib3R does not rely on any patterns and directly estimates camera motion during the optimization process, where the relative motion of the <math id="S4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> camera from pose <math id="S4.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> to pose <math id="S4.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i+1</annotation></semantics></math> is computed as:</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{j,i}=T_{\mathcal{C}_{j,i+1}}^{\mathcal{C}_{j,i}}=T^{\mathcal{C}_{j,i}}_{\mathcal{W}}\cdot T_{\mathcal{C}_{j,i+1}}^{\mathcal{W}}\quad." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.000em;" width="1.000em"></mspace><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{j,i}=T_{\mathcal{C}_{j,i+1}}^{\mathcal{C}_{j,i}}=T^{\mathcal{C}_{j,i}}_{\mathcal{W}}\cdot T_{\mathcal{C}_{j,i+1}}^{\mathcal{W}}\quad.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p5.3" class="ltx_p">Since such estimations of the camera motion lack a consistent metric scale, the standard hand-eye calibration formulation in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E5" title="In IV Calib3R ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) cannot be applied directly.
To overcome this, we explicitly introduce an unknown scale factor <math id="S4.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{j}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{j}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> for each camera <math id="S4.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> mounted on the robot, and incorporate it into the optimization.
A separate scale factor per camera is essential to handle cases where no visual overlap exists between images captured by different cameras.
Without such shared content, the relative scale between their independently reconstructed scenes cannot be inferred from visual correspondences.
The resulting calibration loss for the <math id="S4.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> camera is defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{cal,j}=\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}\left|\left|A_{i}X-XB_{j,i}(\lambda_{j})\right|\right|_{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{cal,j}=\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}\left|\left|A_{i}X-XB_{j,i}(\lambda_{j})\right|\right|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p5.4" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{j,i}(\lambda_{j})=\begin{bmatrix}R_{B_{j,i}}&amp;\lambda_{j}t_{B_{j,i}}\\
\textbf{0}&amp;1\end{bmatrix}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>R</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></msub></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">0</mtext></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{j,i}(\lambda_{j})=\begin{bmatrix}R_{B_{j,i}}&amp;\lambda_{j}t_{B_{j,i}}\\
\textbf{0}&amp;1\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p5.5" class="ltx_p">represents the camera motion with its translation components scaled by the <math id="S4.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> factor.
This formulation ensures alignment between each camera’s motion and the robot’s trajectory, enforcing metric consistency and enabling all camera observations to be expressed in the initial robot reference frame.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">When multiple cameras are mounted on the same robotic system—for instance, in mobile platforms to enhance environmental perception <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>, it is possible to exploit the relative rigid transformation between cameras as additional constraints in the optimization process.
To this end, Calib3R introduces a dedicated <span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">cross-camera</span> consistency loss that jointly optimizes the relative poses between multiple cameras when available.
By leveraging synchronized robot motion and enforcing rigidity constraints, it preserves the fixed inter-camera geometry throughout the trajectory, enabling consistent multi-camera calibration within a unified optimization framework.
As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ IV Calib3R ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> for a multi-camera robot setup, the motion of two cameras <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> between poses <math id="S4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i+1</annotation></semantics></math>
can be expressed using the general hand-eye formulation <math id="S4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AX=XB" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AX=XB</annotation></semantics></math>, as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathcal{C}_{n,i+1}}^{\mathcal{C}_{n,i}}T^{\mathcal{C}_{n}}_{\mathcal{C}_{m}}=T^{\mathcal{C}_{n}}_{\mathcal{C}_{m}}T_{\mathcal{C}_{m,i+1}}^{\mathcal{C}_{m,i}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathcal{C}_{n,i+1}}^{\mathcal{C}_{n,i}}T^{\mathcal{C}_{n}}_{\mathcal{C}_{m}}=T^{\mathcal{C}_{n}}_{\mathcal{C}_{m}}T_{\mathcal{C}_{m,i+1}}^{\mathcal{C}_{m,i}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=T^{\mathcal{C}_{n}}_{\mathcal{C}_{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=T^{\mathcal{C}_{n}}_{\mathcal{C}_{m}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the unknown rigid transformation between cameras <math id="S4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S4.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}=T_{\mathcal{C}_{n,i+1}}^{\mathcal{C}_{n,i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}=T_{\mathcal{C}_{n,i+1}}^{\mathcal{C}_{n,i}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{i}=T_{\mathcal{C}_{m,i+1}}^{\mathcal{C}_{m,i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{i}=T_{\mathcal{C}_{m,i+1}}^{\mathcal{C}_{m,i}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote their respective motions between consecutive robot poses.
Applying this constraint across all images for a camera pair <math id="S4.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C_{n},C_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C_{n},C_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> mounted on the robot yields the following cross-camera loss:</p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{cross}=\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}\left\|T^{\mathcal{C}_{n,i}}_{\mathcal{C}_{n,i+1}}(\lambda_{n})\cdot T^{\mathcal{C}_{n}}_{\mathcal{C}_{m}}-T^{\mathcal{C}_{n}}_{\mathcal{C}_{m}}\cdot T^{\mathcal{C}_{m,i}}_{\mathcal{C}_{m,i+1}}(\lambda_{m})\right\|_{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{cross}=\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}\left\|T^{\mathcal{C}_{n,i}}_{\mathcal{C}_{n,i+1}}(\lambda_{n})\cdot T^{\mathcal{C}_{n}}_{\mathcal{C}_{m}}-T^{\mathcal{C}_{n}}_{\mathcal{C}_{m}}\cdot T^{\mathcal{C}_{m,i}}_{\mathcal{C}_{m,i+1}}(\lambda_{m})\right\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p6.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\mathcal{C}_{n,i}}_{\mathcal{C}_{n,i+1}}(\lambda_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\mathcal{C}_{n,i}}_{\mathcal{C}_{n,i+1}}(\lambda_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\mathcal{C}_{m,i}}_{\mathcal{C}_{m,i+1}}(\lambda_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\mathcal{C}_{m,i}}_{\mathcal{C}_{m,i+1}}(\lambda_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> represent the relative transformation between consecutive poses with their translation components scaled by the corresponding factors <math id="S4.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This cross-camera loss enforces geometric and temporal consistency across cameras by ensuring that their relative motions, remain coherent throughout the robot trajectory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, Calib3R jointly optimizes scene geometry, camera-to-robot calibration parameters, and inter-camera relationships through the following unified loss function:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{Calib3R}=\sum_{j=0}^{M-1}\left(\underbrace{\mathcal{L}_{3D,j}+\mathcal{L}_{2D,j}}_{\text{scene}}+\mathcal{L}_{cal,j}\right)+\sum_{\forall(n,m)}\mathcal{L}_{cross}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>scene</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mo>∀</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{Calib3R}=\sum_{j=0}^{M-1}\left(\underbrace{\mathcal{L}_{3D,j}+\mathcal{L}_{2D,j}}_{\text{scene}}+\mathcal{L}_{cal,j}\right)+\sum_{\forall(n,m)}\mathcal{L}_{cross}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The unified loss function is minimized via gradient descent, simultaneously estimating in a single step all key camera parameters to ensure a consistent, metric-scaled, and physically-constrained reconstruction aligned with the robot’s reference frame.
Specifically, the optimization yields: (i) the pose <math id="S4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathcal{C}_{j,i}}^{\mathcal{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathcal{C}_{j,i}}^{\mathcal{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> for each image <math id="S4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> of each camera <math id="S4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> relative to the initial robot frame, (ii) a scale factor <math id="S4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for each camera’s trajectory, and (iii) the rigid camera-to-robot transformations <math id="S4.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathcal{C}_{j}}^{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathcal{C}_{j}}^{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> for all cameras mounted on the robot.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p">When only a single camera is mounted on the robot (<math id="S4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=1</annotation></semantics></math>), the joint loss <math id="S4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{cross}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{cross}</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes, since there are no camera pairs to compare. In this case, it has no influence on the overall optimization.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><img src="2509.08813v1/calib_scheme_multi_cam.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/348;" width="476" height="348" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 3: </span>Relation of incremental motion of multiple cameras onboard of the same robot formulated as an hand-eye calibration problem where the unknown matrix <math id="S4.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=T_{\mathcal{C}_{m}}^{\mathcal{C}_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=T_{\mathcal{C}_{m}}^{\mathcal{C}_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> describes the relative pose among cameras <math id="S4.F3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S4.F3.1" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">.</p></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experimental Evaluation Protocol</span></h2>

<figure id="S5.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F6.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_bottom" style="width:120.8pt;"><img src="2509.08813v1/images/IMG_3410.jpg" id="S5.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:167/167;" width="167" height="167" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 4: </span>Franka Pattern dataset: Experimental setup with a 7×10 checkerboard (3 cm squares) placed within the robot’s workspace for calibration.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F6.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_bottom" style="width:120.8pt;"><img src="2509.08813v1/images/IMG_3408.jpg" id="S5.F6.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:167/167;" width="167" height="167" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 5: </span>Franka Object dataset: Generic objects are placed in the robot’s workspace to support calibration and scene reconstruction without relying on a pattern.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F6.fig3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_bottom" style="width:90.4pt;"><img src="2509.08813v1/images/IMG_4118.jpg" id="S5.F6.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:125/167;" width="125" height="167" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 6: </span>GraspNet-1Billion dataset: Robot setup with generic objects placed in its workspace <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite>.</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate the effectiveness of the proposed Calib3R method, we designed a comprehensive evaluation protocol encompassing both robotic arms and mobile robot scenarios, demonstrating its applicability across different robotic platforms.
In both contexts, we selected datasets that include at least one scene with a standard calibration pattern, enabling direct and fair comparisons with both traditional calibration methods that explicitly rely on such patterns and more recent pattern-free approaches based on generic image features. In addition, we also assess Calib3R on datasets that do not contain any pattern, to highlight its general applicability in unconstrained and realistic environments.
Our evaluation is focused on two core aspects, as introduced in Sec. <a href="#S4" title="IV Calib3R ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>: the accuracy of camera-to-robot calibration, and the metric scale consistency of the scene geometry.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">V-A</span> </span><span id="S5.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Metrics</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt two quantitative metrics to comprehensively assess the calibration accuracy and the metric scale accuracy of the 3D reconstruction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Calibration accuracy</span> is measured in terms of translation and rotation errors of the estimated camera-to-robot transformation. The translation error is computed as the Euclidean distance between the estimated translation vector <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and the ground-truth vector <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. The rotation error is computed as the angle derived from the axis-angle representation of the relative rotation matrix between the estimated <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and ground-truth <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> rotations, denoted as <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="angle(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">angle(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S5.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{t}=\frac{1}{M}\sum_{i=0}^{M-1}\|t-\hat{t}\|_{2},\quad e_{\theta}=\frac{1}{M}\sum_{i=0}^{M-1}angle(R^{T}\hat{R})\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{t}=\frac{1}{M}\sum_{i=0}^{M-1}\|t-\hat{t}\|_{2},\quad e_{\theta}=\frac{1}{M}\sum_{i=0}^{M-1}angle(R^{T}\hat{R})\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metric scale accuracy</span> is evaluated on datasets that include a checkerboard. All checkerboard corners detected across <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> views from <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> cameras are reprojected into the reconstructed pointcloud and arranged based on the known checkerboard layout. We compute the mean Euclidean distance <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math> between adjacent corners (both row- and column-wise) for each checkerboard detection <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and evaluate the mean reconstructed square size <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> in comparison with the ground-truth square size <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. We report the absolute scale error <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{s}-s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{s}-s</annotation></semantics></math>, the standard deviation <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the relative scale error <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
<table id="S5.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="m_{s}=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=0}^{K-1}d^{(k)},\quad\delta_{s}(\%)=\frac{|m_{s}-s|}{s}\times 100\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo>%</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>s</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>100</mn><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{s}=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=0}^{K-1}d^{(k)},\quad\delta_{s}(\%)=\frac{|m_{s}-s|}{s}\times 100\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This evaluation provides a robust indication of Calib3R’s ability to produce metrically accurate reconstructions, an essential capability for robotic perception and interaction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">V-B</span> </span><span id="S5.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Manipulator-mounted Camera Validation</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Calib3R is validated on three different robotic arm datasets: two collected using our own manipulator setup and one public benchmark.
The Franka Pattern and Franka Object datasets were acquired using a Kinect Azure camera rigidly mounted on a Franka Emika Panda robot arm (see Fig. <a href="#S5.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ V Experimental Evaluation Protocol ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> and Fig. <a href="#S5.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ V Experimental Evaluation Protocol ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). The Franka Pattern dataset consists of 25 robot poses captured in front of a <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7\times 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7\times 10</annotation></semantics></math> checkerboard with 3 cm squares, while the Franka Object dataset replicates the same poses but replaces the checkerboard with generic objects to simulate a pattern-free setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To extend our evaluation to realistic manipulation scenarios, we also include the GraspNet-1Billion benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite>, a large-scale public dataset for robotic grasping (Fig. <a href="#S5.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ V Experimental Evaluation Protocol ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). We randomly select 25 images from scene_0100 to maintain consistency with our experimental protocol.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">V-C</span> </span><span id="S5.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Mobile Robot Validation</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For mobile robots, we evaluate Calib3R on multiple datasets collected across various platforms and diverse environments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The MEMROC dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> includes both real and synthetic sequences captured using a tri-camera setup mounted on a mobile robot observing a <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7\times 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7\times 6</annotation></semantics></math> checkerboard with 10 cm squares (see Fig. <a href="#S7.F12" title="Fig. 12 ‣ VII Results on motion-based calibration ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> and Fig. <a href="#S7.F12" title="Fig. 12 ‣ VII Results on motion-based calibration ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>). This dataset enables direct comparison between pattern-based and pattern-free calibration methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The OpenLORIS dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite> provides single-camera sequences recorded in realistic indoor environments (Fig. <a href="#S7.F12" title="Fig. 12 ‣ VII Results on motion-based calibration ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>), while the CSE Benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite> offers synthetic data generated in NVIDIA Isaac Sim using a mobile robot equipped with a dual-camera configuration (Fig. <a href="#S7.F12" title="Fig. 12 ‣ VII Results on motion-based calibration ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS4.4" class="ltx_text">V-D</span> </span><span id="S5.SS4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Computational Setup</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Experiments with Calib3R and the 3D foundation model-based method were conducted on an NVIDIA A40 GPU with 48 GB of GDDR6 memory. Classical calibration methods were executed on a standard workstation equipped with an Intel Core i7-1280P CPU, 16 GB of RAM, and running Ubuntu 20.04.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VI </span><span id="S6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Results on hand-eye calibration</span></h2>

<figure id="S6.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE I: </span>Average error in the estimated camera-to-robot (hand-eye) transformation, computed using images acquired from 25 robot poses in the Franka Pattern dataset (Fig. <a href="#S5.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ V Experimental Evaluation Protocol ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), Franka Object dataset (Fig. <a href="#S5.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ V Experimental Evaluation Protocol ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) and GraspNet-1Billion (Fig. <a href="#S5.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ V Experimental Evaluation Protocol ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). The Pattern column indicates whether the corresponding method requires a calibration pattern. Traditional method can not be used in Franka Object dataset and GraspNet-1Billion benchmark since it is not involved a calibration pattern.</figcaption>
<table id="S6.T1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S6.T1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S6.T1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Method</span></th>
<th id="S6.T1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S6.T1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text">Pattern</span></th>
