<td id="S6.T1.1.13.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S6.T1.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.023" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.023</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.023</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conducted extensive experiments on Franka Pattern dataset (Fig. <a href="#S5.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ V Experimental Evaluation Protocol ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) to compare classical pattern-based hand-eye calibration methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>, <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, against recent reconstruction-based methods inspired by Zhi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>. Specifically, reconstruction-based methods, including COLMAP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite> and 3D foundation model such as DUSt3R <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, MASt3R-SfM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, and VGGT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite>, were used to estimate camera poses purely through visual geometry. Subsequently, these poses are used to perform hand-eye calibration using the formulation proposed by Zhi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The quantitative results of our experiments, summarized in Table <a href="#S6.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ VI Results on hand-eye calibration ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>, present a thorough comparison of classical pattern-based (✓) and modern pattern-free calibration strategies (✗) by evaluating average translation and rotation errors in the estimated camera-to-robot transformations.
Notably, Calib3R significantly outperforms all reconstruction-based methods that follow a two-step calibration pipeline, such as COLMAP + Calib and DUSt3R + Calib, reducing the translation error by an order of magnitude (1.127 cm compared to 28.210 cm and 24.641 cm, respectively).
Calib3R also outperforms MASt3R-SfM + Calib, achieving nearly a 40% reduction in both translation and rotation errors. Importantly, although both methods are built on MASt3R, the joint optimization strategy of Calib3R clearly demonstrates that a unified pipeline yields more accurate and consistent results.
This underlines the inherent limitations of decoupled approaches, which are more vulnerable to intermediate reconstruction errors and misalignments between stages.
Furthermore, Calib3R surpasses even the strongest reconstruction-based competitor, VGGT + Calib, reducing translation error by over 30%. Among traditional pattern-based methods, Calib3R outperforms all except Evangelista’s reprojection-minimization approach, which achieves a slightly lower translation error of 0.781 cm. However, Calib3R achieves the best rotation accuracy overall—including compared to Evangelista’s method—reaching a remarkably low error of 0.014 radians. This robustness in rotation estimation stems from two key factors: first, rotation is inherently unaffected by scale ambiguity, which remains a challenging component of calibration; and second, unlike checkerboard- or pattern-based methods that rely on a limited set of locally accurate points, our approach leverages 3D foundation models that extract and match visual features densely and globally distributed across the image, resulting in more stable and redundant geometric constraints.
To demonstrate Calib3R’s broad applicability, we evaluated it not only on scenes with calibration patterns, where it performs comparably or better than the state of the art, but also on patternless environments.
Specifically, we tested it on Franka Object dataset (Fig. <a href="#S5.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ V Experimental Evaluation Protocol ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), replacing the checkerboard with arbitrary objects to simulate a realistic scenario without explicit calibration targets.
In this more challenging setting, traditional pattern-based methods are no longer applicable, underscoring the importance of robust pattern-free calibration approaches. Calib3R clearly outperforms all reconstruction-based methods, achieving exceptionally low translation and rotation errors (0.415 cm and 0.011 radians, respectively). Remarkably, these results are even superior to Evangelista’s method tested on Franka Pattern dataset, which had the advantage of using a dedicated calibration pattern.
This highlights a significant advancement in the accuracy and reliability of pattern-free calibration approaches.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, since 3D foundation model-based reconstruction methods are typically more computationally demanding, we restrict our comparison to other foundation model-based approaches and analyze how calibration accuracy varies with the number of input images in the Franka Object dataset, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S6.F7" title="Fig. 7 ‣ VI Results on hand-eye calibration ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.08813v1/h2e_results.svg" id="S6.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/330;" width="476" height="330"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 7: </span>Camera-to-robot transformation accuracy as a function of the number of input images. Starting from 25 images, 2 random images were removed at each step down to a minimum of 5.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this experiment, we started with the initial set of 25 images captured by the single-camera system and progressively removed 2 random images at each step until reaching a minimum of 5 images—a particularly low number for calibration. The results show that Calib3R consistently outperforms all other methods in both rotation and translation accuracy. Specifically, it achieves a translation error below 5 cm and a rotation error under 0.04 radians with as few as 5 images. Notably, with just 15 images, Calib3R maintains a translation error below 1 cm.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To further assess the robustness of the proposed method, we evaluated its calibration accuracy on the publicly available GraspNet-1Billion dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite>. Similarly to the Franka Object dataset we limited the comparison to pattern-free methods. The resulting hand-eye calibration accuracy is reported in the last column of Table <a href="#S6.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ VI Results on hand-eye calibration ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>.
Calib3R achieves superior performance even on the challenging GraspNet dataset, although the results are slightly less accurate than those obtained on the Franka Object dataset. This gap may be attributed to the limited variability in robot motion within GraspNet sequences, which primarily involve grasping tasks. The resulting camera viewpoints are predominantly top-down, with minimal rotation—particularly around the roll and pitch axes. This lack of diverse and distinctive motion trajectories likely limits the observability of the unknown transformation, thereby reducing the effectiveness of calibration methods that rely on motion diversity to achieve high accuracy. A qualitative example of the metric-scaled 3D reconstruction using a scene from the GraspNet-1Billion dataset is shown in Fig. <a href="#S6.F8" title="Fig. 8 ‣ VI Results on hand-eye calibration ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. The reconstruction was generated using 15 RGB images, and it clearly shows how all elements are accurately aligned with the robot’s base reference frame.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.08813v1/images/grasp.png" id="S6.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/363;" width="476" height="363" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 8: </span>Metric-scaled 3D reconstruction of a scene from the GraspNet dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite>, generated using only 15 RGB images. Both the estimated camera poses and the corresponding robot poses are expressed in the robot’s base frame by applying the kinematics from the initial end-effector pose (at step 0).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p">As a final step, we assessed the metric scale accuracy of the reconstructed 3D scene on the Franka Pattern dataset, focusing on Calib3R and recent dense reconstruction-based approaches.
Note that COLMAP was excluded from this analysis, as it does not produce reconstructions that are sufficiently dense or complete to reliably project the checkerboard corners into 3D space.
The evaluation of this metric is reported in Table <a href="#S6.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ VI Results on hand-eye calibration ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE II: </span>Metric scale accuracy of 3D reconstruction on Franka Pattern dataset.
The table reports the mean scale error, the standard deviation and the percentage scale error relative to the true checkerboard square size of 3 cm.
Lower values indicate higher metric consistency.</figcaption>
<table id="S6.T2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S6.T2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S6.T2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Method</span></th>
