<td id="S6.T2.1.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S6.T2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.67" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.67</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.67</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p7.1" class="ltx_p">As clearly shown, Calib3R achieves the highest metric consistency among all methods, significantly reducing the relative scale error of its closest competitor VGGT + Calib (3.67% vs. 5.67%).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VII </span><span id="S7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Results on motion-based calibration</span></h2>

<figure id="S7.F12" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S7.F12.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_bottom" style="width:89.7pt;"><img src="2509.08813v1/images/real_memroc.png" id="S7.F12.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:124/124;" width="124" height="124" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 9: </span>Real-world experimental setup of the MEMROC dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S7.F12.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_bottom" style="width:89.7pt;"><img src="2509.08813v1/images/synth_memroc.jpg" id="S7.F12.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:124/124;" width="124" height="124" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 10: </span>Synthetic experimental setup of the MEMROC dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S7.F12.fig3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_bottom" style="width:55.2pt;"><img src="2509.08813v1/images/robot_segway_openloris.png" id="S7.F12.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:77/124;" width="77" height="124" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 11: </span>Robot from the OpenLORIS dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite>.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S7.F12.fig4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_bottom" style="width:93.2pt;"><img src="2509.08813v1/images/cse_benchmark_carter.png" id="S7.F12.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:129/124;" width="129" height="124" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 12: </span>Experimental setup from the CSE Benchmark dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite>.</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the generalizability and robustness of Calib3R in mobile robot scenarios, we performed extensive experiments with several state-of-the-art calibration methods. These approaches are designed exclusively for calibration and do not include 3D reconstruction, which is typically handled by separate systems.
To the best of our knowledge, no existing method jointly performs both calibration and reconstruction in mobile robot setups.
Therefore, we evaluate Calib3R against the following calibration-only baselines:</p>
<ul id="S7.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S7.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Motion-based methods:</span> Zuniga et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite> (pattern-free ✗) and MEMROC and its variant Joint-MEMROC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> (pattern-based ✓);</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Learning-based method:</span> SensorX2Car by Yan et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite> (pattern-free ✗).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note that, a well-known limitation in mobile robot calibration is the z-unobservability problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>. Since the robot moves on a planar surface, the z-component of the transformation cannot be inferred from motion alone. In accordance with existing literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>, we estimate this z-translation component post-calibration using the camera’s height from the ground.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To this end, we propose a VLM-based ground plane estimation pipeline. Specifically, we employ Grounding DINO<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>]</cite> and SAM 2<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>]</cite> to segment the floor in each RGB image using natural language prompts. These segmentations are back-projected to the final 3D pointcloud and the final z-component of the camera-to-robot transformation is estimated as the average height of the camera across <math id="S7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> all frames:</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S7.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}d_{i}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}d_{i}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This prompt-based approach proves more robust than traditional geometric methods (e.g., RANSAC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>]</cite>), which are prone to misidentifying other planar surfaces such as tables or walls as the ground.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S7.T3" title="TABLE III ‣ VII Results on motion-based calibration ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a> summarizes the results across the following datasets: real-world and synthetic MEMROC datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, OpenLORIS<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite>, and the CSE benchmark<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite>.
The MEMROC datasets feature visible checkerboard calibration patterns, allowing the use of pattern-based methods such as MEMROC and Joint-MEMROC. In contrast, OpenLORIS and CSE do not contain such patterns, making pattern-based methods inapplicable.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE III: </span>Average error in the estimated camera-to-robot transformation across the real-world and synthetic MEMROC datasets, as well as the OpenLORIS and CSE benchmarks, computed over 25 robot poses. The Pattern column indicates whether the method requires a visible calibration pattern.</figcaption>
