<td id="S7.T3.1.7.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">2.432</td>
<td id="S7.T3.1.7.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r">0.006</td>
<td id="S7.T3.1.7.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S7.T3.1.7.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3.419</span></td>
<td id="S7.T3.1.7.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r"><span id="S7.T3.1.7.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.033</span></td>
<td id="S7.T3.1.7.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S7.T3.1.7.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.723</span></td>
<td id="S7.T3.1.7.10" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S7.T3.1.7.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.005</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S7.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Calib3R demonstrates strong and consistent performance on both the real-world and synthetic versions of the MEMROC dataset. On the real-world data, it achieves results comparable to the best pattern-based method, Joint-MEMROC, with only a 12% increase in translation error (3.725 cm vs. 3.325 cm), while improving rotation accuracy by approximately 32%, as discussed in Sec. <a href="#S6" title="VI Results on hand-eye calibration ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a>. As expected, MEMROC and Joint-MEMROC achieve superior performance on the synthetic dataset, thanks to their use of highly accurate relative camera poses obtained via the calibration pattern. In such ideal conditions, free of noise, pattern detection is nearly perfect, resulting in metrically precise camera poses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Among pattern-free methods, Calib3R clearly stands out. The motion-based method by Zuniga et al.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite> shows significantly higher errors, particularly on the real-world MEMROC, with translation errors exceeding 10 cm and rotation errors above 0.1 rad. The learning-based approach SensorX2Car<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite> does not estimate translation and achieves low rotation accuracy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p9.1" class="ltx_p">We further evaluated Calib3R on the OpenLORIS<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite> and CSE benchmark<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite> datasets (last two columns of Table <a href="#S7.T3" title="TABLE III ‣ VII Results on motion-based calibration ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>).
On both datasets, Calib3R substantially outperforms the alternatives, especially in translation, achieving errors of 3.419 cm and 2.723 cm—an order of magnitude lower than those of Zuniga’s method (35.126 cm and 17.123 cm), which is impractical for real-world robotic applications. SensorX2Car remains limited by its lack of translation estimation. In rotation as well, Calib3R achieves strong results outperforming all other state-of-the-art pattern-free approaches.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p10.1" class="ltx_p">As in the robotic arm scenario, we evaluated the calibration methods on the real MEMROC dataset by progressively reducing the number of images per camera, from 25 down to 5. The results, shown in Fig. <a href="#S7.F13" title="Fig. 13 ‣ VII Results on motion-based calibration ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>, reveal that Calib3R significantly outperforms other pattern-free methods, maintaining high accuracy even with fewer than 10 images per camera.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F13" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.08813v1/motion_based_calib_vs_images.svg" id="S7.F13.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/300;" width="476" height="300"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 13: </span>Camera-to-robot transformation accuracy as a function of the number of input images. Starting from 25 images, 2 random images were removed at each step down to a minimum of 5.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S7.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p11.1" class="ltx_p">A qualitative example of a 3D reconstruction using the MEMROC dataset is shown in Fig. <a href="#S7.F14" title="Fig. 14 ‣ VII Results on motion-based calibration ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>, generated from only 15 RGB images per camera. The figure illustrates the estimated camera poses alongside the corresponding mobile robot poses at each acquisition step. The reconstruction is metric-scaled and aligned with the initial position of the robot’s base reference frame, accurately reflecting the robot’s pose at each point in the trajectory.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F14" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.08813v1/images/memroc_image.png" id="S7.F14.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/234;" width="476" height="234" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 14: </span>Metric-scaled 3D reconstruction of the environment using 15 images per camera from a MEMROC dataset sequence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. The reconstruction includes the estimated camera poses and the corresponding 15 robot poses, all expressed with respect to the first robot pose reference frame.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S7.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p12.1" class="ltx_p">The real-world MEMROC dataset was also used to evaluate the metric scale accuracy of the 3D scene reconstructed exclusively by Calib3R, since other methods focus solely on camera calibration and do not produce any reconstruction. The evaluation follows the same protocol adopted for robotic arm setups (see Sec. <a href="#S5" title="V Experimental Evaluation Protocol ‣ Calib3R: A 3D Foundation Model for Multi-Camera to Robot Calibration and 3D Metric-Scaled Scene Reconstruction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a>). Specifically, we compute the mean reconstructed square size <math id="S7.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, its standard deviation <math id="S7.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the relative percentage error across all detectable checkerboard corners.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Calib3R achieves a highly accurate metric reconstruction, with a mean scale error of <math id="S7.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{s}-s=0.48" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.48</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{s}-s=0.48</annotation></semantics></math> cm, a standard deviation of <math id="S7.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{s}=0.025" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.025</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{s}=0.025</annotation></semantics></math>, and a percentage scale error <math id="S7.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{s}=4.8\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4.8</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{s}=4.8\%</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VIII </span><span id="S8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CONCLUSIONS</span></h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we presented Calib3R, a unified calibration method for both single- and multi-camera setups mounted on robotic arms or mobile robots. Calib3R leverages the 3D foundation model MASt3R to estimate the relative pose of one or more cameras with respect to the robot’s reference frame.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our method requires only a set of RGB images acquired from the robot-mounted cameras and the corresponding robot poses obtained via kinematics or odometry. Unlike traditional approaches that treat calibration and 3D reconstruction as separate tasks, Calib3R performs both simultaneously, producing accurate camera-to-robot calibration and a metric scale 3D reconstruction of the environment aligned to the robot reference frame. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first unified approach that generalizes across both robotic arms and mobile platforms, as well as across single- and multi-camera configurations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We carried out extensive experiments using both real-world and synthetic datasets, demonstrating that Calib3R is highly accurate, robust, and effective even in low-data conditions with fewer than 10 images per camera. Notably, we found that traditional calibration patterns can be replaced by generic task-relevant objects, achieving camera-to-robot transformation errors below 0.5 cm in robotic arm setups, outperforming conventional methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As 3D foundation models continue to advance, future work will focus on enabling real-time calibration and reconstruction, and extending the method to handle dynamic environments.</p>
</div>
</section>
